; ONLY-IN-UDON — the register of things you can do well in udon that you
; can't do well elsewhere. Seeded 2026-07-28 from Joseph's aside: "the body
; of 'things you can do well in udon that you can't do well elsewhere' will
; almost certainly grow and want to be tracked."
;
; Honesty rule for every entry: "can't do well elsewhere" is earned by
; comparison, never by assertion — each record names the nearest-elsewhere
; and states the delta. :status candidate until the comparison has actually
; been run; measured evidence cited where it exists. Pre-validation
; register; nothing here is positioning copy yet.

|capability[structured-quote-with-provenance] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere markdown blockquote / HTML cite
  An attributed quote as structure: |quote :src joseph :date ... — who,
  when, from-where as queryable edges with default viewing derivable.
  Markdown's blockquote has styling as its only slot, so every attribution
  ever written is a display hack invisible to query; HTML's cite is
  semantic in name and unqueried in practice. Delta: metadata-affordances
  with default viewing vs view-only. Demand evidence: the 01-ideation
  gathered corpus (~270 artifacts) is provenance-quotes forced into YAML
  frontmatter workarounds.

|capability[mechanical-defacto-schema-extraction] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere JSON schema-inference / markdown convention-guessing
  The markers make the schema's vocabulary explicit, so de-facto-schema
  extraction reduces to statistics over a known vocabulary — two inference
  problems reduced to one. JSON inference works but records-only (no prose
  interleaving to preserve); markdown inference must first guess what even
  carries structure. Evidence: the vivarium specimen (conforming dense
  record from grep + one exemplar); probe 8 (schema seed) is the
  measurement.

|capability[schema-over-comments] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere none at the format layer; javadoc/lint tooling partially
  Comments are first-class model items (carried, never interpreted), so
  "are the comments here conformant / present / well-placed" is newly
  askable at the schema layer. Every other data format discards comments
  before validation, so there is nothing to validate; the partial
  precedents (javadoc requirements, missing-docs lints) live in
  language-specific tooling, not the document format. From the schema
  seed's tier table (§5.1).

|capability[judged-from-first-byte] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere HTML5 error recovery / tree-sitter
  Recognition is total — any finite UTF-8 input maps to a Document,
  schema-independently — so there is no unparseable-therefore-unjudgeable
  state and continuous schema judgment is mechanical, not aspirational.
  HTML5 recovers but mutates toward its own model; tree-sitter keeps a
  tree alive but is parse-only with no judgment layer. Delta: keep-
  everything + severity-defined-by-loss beneath a non-invasive judge.

|capability[closure-without-loss] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere JSON Schema additionalProperties:false
  Closed-world judgment with zero data loss: close the world in the
  verdict while the document keeps everything. Elsewhere closure is
  enforced by rejection or discard, so it trades against extensibility
  and data preservation; here the trade dissolves (schema seed, corrected
  §1.1 — enforcement-by-removal is what is foreclosed, not closure).

|capability[prose-structure-interleave-addressable-boundary] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere MDX / XML mixed content
  Structure and prose interleave at any depth with a *precise, addressable*
  boundary (the content base — geometry, not delimiters). MDX has the
  parse-ambiguity seam between prose and component; XML mixed content works
  but at closing-tag ceremony that killed its prose ergonomics. The
  no-island-boundary property is what makes schema-over-prose newly
  tractable here (schema seed §5.3).

|capability[slug-identity-with-ref-guard] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere markdown wikilinks
  Identity ([key]) and reference (@) are typed structure, so dangling-
  reference checking is mechanical and cross-file. Wikilinks give links
  without typed identity; nothing distinguishes a rename from a removal.
  Measured evidence: the vivarium ref-guard caught a renamed slug within
  the hour, main went red, fixed same day (I5 field report, 2026-07-28) —
  "markdown couldn't have given us that without inventing worse
  conventions."

|capability[headline-density-under-line-tools] :status candidate :date 2026-07-28
  :nearest-elsewhere YAML frontmatter / JSONL
  One line carries identity + classification + state as readable structure
  (|decision[slug] :date :by :status :topic), so grep and line tools
  answer real queries without a parser. Frontmatter separates metadata
  from the content it describes; JSONL is line-queryable but unreadable.
  Evidence: the vivarium day-report ("reads beautifully under grep" —
  pulled a settled, dated answer in one look mid-design); the estate
  convention attributes-first-sameline exists *because* this works.
